[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Quirks that had to be preserved, and were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`fread`, not `fgets` or `scanf`.** Reading crosses newlines and whitespace
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and continues to EOF or 1000 bytes. Verified by `reads_across_newlines` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `input_delivered_in_several_chunks`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Read as raw bytes, scanned as a C string.** `fread` never appends a NUL and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the buffer is zero-initialised, so an *embedded* NUL byte silently hides the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    rest of the input. `AA\0xxAA` prints `A: 2` / `x: 0`, not `A: 4` / `x: 2`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **The `fread` return value is discarded.** An unreadable stdin is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    indistinguishable from empty input: the program still prints `A: 0` / `x: 0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    writes nothing to stderr and exits 0. The Rust code must swallow the I/O
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    error rather than reporting it. This is the one behaviour that only the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `unreadable_stdin_is_ignored` test detects.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **Hard 1000-byte cap.** Input 1001+ bytes long is silently truncated; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    1001st byte cannot be counted even when it is an `A` or an `x`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **Byte comparison, not character comparison.** `strchr` compares single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    bytes, so no multi-byte or non-ASCII sequence can ever match `A` or `x`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    invalid UTF-8 passes through without complaint. The Rust side must not touch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `String`/`str` on the input path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **No error paths at all.** stderr is always empty and the exit status is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    always 0, for every input including the pathological ones.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Undefined behaviour in the C, and how it was handled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m When stdin supplies exactly 1000 or more bytes, the buffer is filled completely
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and **`in` is not NUL-terminated**, so `strchr` reads past the end of the array.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is undefined behaviour in the C, which cannot simply be "translated".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rather than guess, the observed behaviour was measured. The C was rebuilt at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-O0`, `-O2`, and `-O3 -fstack-protector-strong` in addition to the CMake build,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and each was run repeatedly and with the stack shifted (a 60 KB environment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m variable, and 500 extra environment variables). All builds and all layouts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reported `A: 1000` — the byte immediately after the array is consistently zero,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so the scan stops exactly at the array end.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/main.rs` reproduces this by treating position 1000 as an implicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terminator (`position(|&b| b == 0).unwrap_or(BUF_SIZE)`), which is the behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observed from the C and is also memory-safe. Pinned by `exactly_at_capacity`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `one_byte_over_capacity` and `capacity_reached_then_nul`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Suite validation (fault injection)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m To confirm the suite would actually catch a bad translation, six faults were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m injected into `src/main.rs` one at a time; each was caught, and the original
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m source was restored afterwards (verified byte-identical).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Injected fault | Caught by |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `BUF_SIZE` 1000 → 999 | `capacity_reached_then_nul`, `far_over_capacity_is_truncated`, … |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `s = found + 1` → `found + 2` (skips adjacent matches) | `adjacent_matches`, `one_byte_over_capacity`, … |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Scan the whole read buffer instead of stopping at the first NUL | `leading_nul_yields_empty_string`, `binary_payload_all_byte_values`, … |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `"A: {}\n"` → `"A:{}\n"` (dropped space) | `output_format_is_exact`, `just_under_capacity`, … |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `exit(1)` instead of `return 0` | every test — all three streams are asserted |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Report the stdin read error to stderr and exit 1 | `unreadable_stdin_is_ignored` (**only** this test) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The last row is why the error-path cases matter: that fault leaves stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m identical on ordinary input, so a stdout-only suite would have passed it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
