Since "no mismatches" is also what a suite that measures nothing reports, I injected six faults into the Rust source one at a time — wrong buffer size, skipping adjacent matches, ignoring the embedded NUL, a dropped space in the format string, a nonzero exit, and reporting the stdin read error — and confirmed each was caught, then restored the source byte-identically. That last fault was caught by only one test: the C discards the [38;5;10mfread[0m return value, so an unreadable stdin must still print [38;5;10mA: 0 / x: 0[0m and exit 0. On ordinary input that bug leaves stdout untouched, so a stdout-only suite would have passed it.[0m[0m
